          "text": "Verified empirically + one search. Answer:\n\n## `packageManager` is not enough — but `only-allow` isn't the right guard either\n\n**What `packageManager` actually buys you today:**\n\n1. **CI**: load-bearing. `pnpm/action-setup@v6` is pinned with no `version:` input in all 8 workflow jobs — it reads `packageManager` to pick pnpm 10.34.4. Keep it.\n2. **pnpm→pnpm version pinning**: also load-bearing, and *not* via corepack. pnpm self-manages (`manage-package-manager-versions`, default on). Proven just now: global `pnpm -v` is 11.18.0, but inside the repo it resolves to 10.34.4.\n3. **Cross-manager enforcement**: **zero**. Corepack is opt-in (`corepack enable`) and is [no longer distributed with Node 25+](https://socket.dev/blog/node-js-tsc-votes-to-stop-distributing-corepack) — TSC voted to stop shipping it; it stays in 24 LTS as experimental only. Your `engines.node` is `>=22.12`, so the fraction of contributors who even *have* corepack shrinks every month. This gap widens on its own.\n\n**The failure it leaves open is silent, not loud.** Root `package.json` has no `workspaces` field, so `npm install` doesn't link `examples/*` or `packages/storyproof` at all — it just installs 4 root devDeps, drops a `package-lock.json` (not gitignored), then trips over `prepare: pnpm --filter storyproof build`. The contributor gets a confusing mid-install error, not \"wrong package manager.\" For a repo whose CONTRIBUTING.md actively courts outside PRs and whose examples *are* the onboarding surface, that's worth 12 lines.\n\n**Use a local preinstall script, not `npx only-allow`.** On the npm path `node_modules` is empty, so `npx` does a registry fetch of an unpinned package and executes it — network dependency and supply-chain surface on the *failure* path, in a repo that already pins turbo exactly and SHA-pins every action. A local script has none of that and can print a message pointing at CONT********.md.\n\nDetection is sound — I probed it:\n\n```\nnpm  → n