          "text": "Checked what the reference class actually does, not what sounds right.\n\n## Convention is `npx only-allow pnpm`, verbatim\n\nEvery pnpm-workspace OSS repo in your neighbourhood uses the identical string in root `package.json`:\n\n| Repo | preinstall |\n|---|---|\n| [vitejs/vite](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/package.json) | `npx only-allow pnpm` |\n| [vuejs/core](https://github.com/vuejs/core/blob/main/package.json) | `npx only-allow pnpm` |\n| [vitejs/vite-plugin-react](https://github.com/vitejs/vite-plugin-react/blob/main/package.json) | `npx only-allow pnpm` |\n| [vitejs/vite-plugin-vue](https://github.com/vitejs/vite-plugin-vue/blob/main/package.json) | `npx only-allow pnpm` |\n| [supabase/supabase](https://github.com/supabase/supabase/blob/master/packages/config/package.json) | `npx only-allow pnpm` (in *every* workspace package) |\n\nLocal-script variants exist but are the minority, and they're all repos needing more than manager detection: [n8n](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/package.json) `scripts/block-npm-install.js`, [Roo-Code](https://github.com/RooCodeInc/Roo-Code/blob/main/package.json) `scripts/bootstrap.mjs`, [learnGitBranching](https://github.com/pcottle/learnGitBranching/blob/main/package.json) `check-package-manager.js`, NVID********Claw `check-node-version.js`.\n\nNote the storybook data point cuts the other way and is worth knowing: **storybook itself uses yarn 4** with `packageManager` and no preinstall guard at all.\n\n## This revises what I told you\n\nMy supply-chain objection to `npx only-allow` was overweighted. Registry metadata:\n\n```\nonly-allow@1.2.2 — github.com/pnpm/only-allow — maintainer: zkochan (pnpm's author)\ndeps: { \"which-pm-runs\": \"1.1.0\" }   ← exactly pinned, one dep\n6 versions total since inception, last modified 2025-11-28\n```\n\nIt's a pnpm-org package by pnpm's maintainer with one exactly-pinned dependency. That's about as small a surface as a preinstall guard gets. My \"unpinned third-party code executes pre-install\" framing was accurate in mechanism, misleading in magnitude.\n\n## What I'd actually do now\n\n```json\n\"preinstall\": \"npx only-allow pnpm\",\n```\n\nRoot only — `private: true`, and it must not land in `packages/storyproof` (a dependency's preinstall runs on consumer install) or `examples/*` (CI's consumer job `cp -r`s one out and installs there).\n\nThe two real residual costs, so you decide with them in view:\n\n1. **`npx` runs on the happy path too.** On a clean clone `node_modules` is empty at preinstall, so pnpm-users and all 8 CI jobs pay one registry fetch (~5 KB, cached in `~/.npm/_npx` after first hit). A registry hiccup then fails `pnpm install`. Vite and Vue live with this.\n2. **Version drift.** `npx only-allow` resolves latest. Your repo SHA-pins every action, pins turbo exactly, and runs a `minimumReleaseAge` policy — an unpinned floating resolve is inconsistent with that posture.\n\nIf #2 bothers you (it should, given the rest of the repo), `npx only-allow@1.2.2 pnpm` buys the pin without leaving the convention. Slightly off-convention, defensible in one comment, and dependabot won't bump it — you'd own that manually.\n\nMy call: **`npx only-allow pnpm`, unpinned, matching vite/vue.** Convention has value here precisely because outside contributors recognise the error message. Pin it only if you'd rather be consistent with your own pinning discipline than with the ecosystem.\n\n`build:` commit, no changeset. Want me to implement it?",
